{"id":4420,"date":"2021-12-06T15:24:02","date_gmt":"2021-12-06T23:24:02","guid":{"rendered":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/?post_type=lesson&#038;p=4420"},"modified":"2025-01-31T13:42:27","modified_gmt":"2025-01-31T21:42:27","slug":"3-4-gyroscope-cde","status":"publish","type":"lesson","link":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/lesson\/3-4-gyroscope-cde\/","title":{"rendered":"3.4: Gyroscope"},"content":{"rendered":"<div class=\"wpb-content-wrapper\"><p>[vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2213b754003\" name=\"6a2213b754003\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 1<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Before You Fly<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text css=&#8221;.vc_custom_1730227373096{background-color: #ffdddd !important;border-radius: 3px !important;}&#8221;]<\/p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" class=\" wp-image-11766 alignleft\" src=\"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/2023\/06\/icon_warning-150x150-2-84x85.png\" alt=\"\" width=\"106\" height=\"107\" \/><\/p>\n<p data-pm-slice=\"1 1 []\"><strong>PRE-FLIGHT WARNING<\/strong><\/p>\n<p data-pm-slice=\"1 1 []\">Our drones are durable, yet not indestructible. Please exercise caution while piloting your drone. To prolong the longevity of your drone, avoid flying into walls or other objects. Prior to takeoff, please review lesson <em>0.2 \u201cBefore You Fly\u201d.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2213b75413c\" name=\"6a2213b75413c\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 2<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Goals and Steps to Success<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]Goals:<\/p>\n<p>By the end of this lesson, I will be able to:<\/p>\n<ol>\n<li>Describe how a gyroscope works<\/li>\n<li>Explain how a gyroscope reports movements<\/li>\n<li>Explain the difference between displacement and angular rotation<\/li>\n<li>Describe how gyroscope values can be used to improve the accuracy of flight movements<\/li>\n<li>Create programs that use gyroscope values to control flight movements<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p>Steps to Success:<\/p>\n<ol>\n<li>What is a Gyroscope?<\/li>\n<li>Angular Rotation and Displacement<\/li>\n<li>Getting Gyroscope Angles<\/li>\n<li>Put it in a Loop!<\/li>\n<li>Turn 90 Degrees<\/li>\n<li>Gyro with LED<\/li>\n<li>Using Gyroscope with Functions<\/li>\n<li>Turn_degree()<\/li>\n<li>Challenge 1: A More Perfect Polygon<\/li>\n<li>Challenge 2: Plinko<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2213b7541a3\" name=\"6a2213b7541a3\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 3<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>What is a Gyroscope?<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]When you think of a <strong>gyroscope <\/strong>(gyro), you may be thinking of a mounted, spinning wheel that stays balanced in the same orientation, no matter which direction you turn or flip the gyroscope. <em>How?<\/em> This can be explained by conservation of momentum. While you may not have heard of this term before, you have experienced this. For example, if you have ever spun around on an office chair, you probably noticed you spin faster when you bring your arms and legs in. Since momentum has to be conserved, your speed increases when you bring your mass (arms and legs) closer to you.[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;1971&#8243; img_size=&#8221;large&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;][vc_column_text]In electronics, gyroscopes don\u2019t look like this. They are tiny chips that also use motion to detect changes in orientation. They use a concept called the Coriolis Effect by vibrating a plate on top of a chip. When the phone or device moves, it causes the vibrating disc to slide. This movement changes the electrical current transmitted to the sensor, which is then translated into a digital value. We know it&#8217;s hard to believe, but the tiny microchips you see on the CoDrone EDU have moving parts that are affected by movement![\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2213b754221\" name=\"6a2213b754221\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 4<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Angular Rotation and Displacement<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Gyroscopes are necessary to stabilize your drone while flying, since many factors could cause your drone to drift off course. A gyroscope can measure how far and in which direction your drone is tilting or rotating. Using some math and <\/span>algorithms<span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">, a drone can try to correct any unwanted <\/span><b>orientations<\/b><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\"> to remain stable.<br \/>\n<\/span><\/p>\n<p>There are typically two types of movement we are concerned with when navigating our drone:<\/p>\n<p><strong>Angular Rotation \u2014 <\/strong>this refers to the movement around the drone&#8217;s X, Y, and Z axes. The drone tilting to the left or right, forward or backward are all examples of angular rotation.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Displacement \u2014 <\/strong>this refers to the movement of the drone&#8217;s position from one location to another. For example, changing height would be changing the drone&#8217;s vertical displacement.<\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">There are three orientations that we are concerned with when we are flying a drone for stability: roll, pitch, and yaw. The gyro sensor in your CoDrone will return values from 0 to 180 degrees, positive or negative, that correspond to the degrees the drone rotated in each axis. If you have trouble remembering which way is positive and which way is negative, think of the flight movements!<\/span>[\/vc_column_text][vc_images_carousel images=&#8221;6144,6145,6146&#8243; img_size=&#8221;medium&#8221;][vc_column_text]<span style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><em>Did you notice that throttle is not included?<\/em> When a drone flies straight up or down, the change in position is not an <\/span><b>angular<\/b><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\"> (turning) displacement but a vertical displacement. The gyroscope does not measure vertical displacement. That&#8217;s a job for the height sensor!<\/span>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2213b75427b\" name=\"6a2213b75427b\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 5<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Getting Gyroscope Angles<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]Turn on your drone and place it flat on a table. Since there are three axes, each function will return the degrees that CoDrone EDU is tilting in the roll, pitch, and yaw directions. This is why you are seeing three numbers!<\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"python\">from codrone_edu.drone import *\r\n\r\ndrone = Drone()\r\ndrone.pair()\r\n\r\nx_angle  = drone.get_angle_x()\r\ny_angle = drone.get_angle_y()\r\nz_angle = drone.get_angle_z()\r\n\r\nprint(x_angle)\r\nprint(y_angle)\r\nprint(z_angle)\r\n\r\ndrone.close()<\/pre>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2213b7542d8\" name=\"6a2213b7542d8\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 6<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Put it in a Loop!<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">It is not helpful to take one snapshot of the angle data because you can&#8217;t see how the angles change. Set up a for loop that will read the drone\u2019s angles 900 times. This sounds like a lot, but this will give enough time to rotate the drone in every direction to pick up its angle readings.<\/span><\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Your program should look something like this:<\/span><\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"python\">from codrone_edu.drone import *\u00a0\r\n\r\ndrone = Drone()\r\ndrone.pair()\r\n\r\n\r\nfor i in range(900):\r\n\r\n    x_angle = drone.get_angle_x()\r\n    y_angle = drone.get_angle_y()\r\n    z_angle = drone.get_angle_z()\r\n    print(x_angle)\r\n    print(y_angle)\r\n    print(z_angle)\r\n\r\nprint(\"Done\")\r\n\r\ndrone.close()<\/pre>\n<p>When you run your code, your drone will not fly so try picking it up and moving it in different directions slowly to see the numbers change. You will notice that sometimes the numbers are positive and sometimes they are negative. Remember that left roll, backward pitch, or right yaw will result in negative degrees. When you turn beyond 180 degrees, the value will switch to -180.[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2213b75433f\" name=\"6a2213b75433f\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 7<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Turn 90 Degrees<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">You\u2019ve probably noticed that you can\u2019t program your drone to fly a specific amount of degrees. Up until now, you could only use trial and error with the power and number of seconds. Sometimes this method is great, but for things that need precise angles, like flying in a shape, it doesn\u2019t work well.<\/span><\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Fear not! With your new knowledge about CoDrone EDU\u2019s gyroscope, you can create a function that will have the drone turn a specific number of degrees to the right or left. It uses information from the gyro sensor to make more accurate turns.\u00a0\u00a0<\/span><\/p>\n<p>Let&#8217;s start with a 90-degree turn (perfect for squares!). Think about how you would write code to keep turning <em>while<\/em> the angle reading is still greater than &#8211;<em>90 (since drone is turning right)<\/em>. <em><strong>Hint:<\/strong> You need to use a while loop to check the yaw angle.<\/em>[\/vc_column_text][vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"python\">from codrone_edu.drone import * \r\n\r\ndrone = Drone()\r\ndrone.pair() \r\n\r\ndrone.takeoff() \r\nz_angle = drone.get_angle_z() \r\n\r\nwhile z_angle &gt; -90:\r\n    drone.set_yaw(-20) # Turn right using negative power!\r\n    drone.move() # No parameters since we need to move \"forever\" \r\n    z_angle = drone.get_angle_z() # Update the angle \r\n\r\ndrone.land() \r\nprint(\"Done\")\r\ndrone.close()<\/pre>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2213b7543df\" name=\"6a2213b7543df\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 8<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Gyro with LED<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Your challenge for this part: write a program that will change CoDrone EDU\u2019s LED colors as the drone turns! You can pick whatever colors you like, but we used:<\/span><\/p>\n<ul>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Red for 0\u201390 degrees<\/span><\/li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Yellow for 90\u2013180 degrees<\/span><\/li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Green for -180 to -90 degrees<\/span><\/li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Blue for -90 to 0 degrees<\/span><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Add a <code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">sleep()<\/code> command for 1 second and reset the drone\u2019s sensors.<\/span><\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Add a for loop that repeats 100 times and will get the drone\u2019s yaw with the \u00a0<code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">get_angle_z()<\/code>\u00a0<\/span><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">function and then store it to a variable called \u00a0<code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">angle<\/code>.<\/span><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><br \/>\n<\/span><\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Next, write an if statement that will turn the CoDrone EDU\u2019s LED red if the angle is greater than 0 and less than 90. Be careful with this one! Negative numbers are being used in this program, so you do need to include greater than 0 in this conditional.<\/span><\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Do the same for the other three angle settings, but use else if statements instead. Finally, include an else statement that will turn the drone\u2019s LED white.<\/span><\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Include a <code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">sleep()<\/code> command for .05 seconds and then write a <strong>print<\/strong> command that will display as \u201cDone!\u201d<\/span><\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Note:<\/strong> this program doesn\u2019t include any takeoff, landing, or flight commands! As a challenge, add those in yourself.<\/span><\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"python\">from codrone_edu.drone import *\r\n\r\nimport time\r\n\r\ndrone = Drone()\r\n\r\ndrone.pair()\r\n\r\n\r\ntime.sleep(1)\r\n\r\n\r\nfor i in range(100):\r\n\r\n    angle = drone.get_angle_z()\r\n\r\n    if angle &gt; 0 and angle &lt; 90:\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0    drone.LED_color(255,0,0,100) # red\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 elif angle &gt; 90 and angle &lt; 180:\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 drone.LED_color(255,255,0,100) # yellow\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 elif angle &gt; -180 and angle &lt; -90:\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 drone.LED_color(0,255,0,100) # green\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 elif angle &gt; -90 and angle &lt; 0:\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 drone.LED_color(0,0,255,100) # blue\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 else:\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0drone.LED_color(255,255,255,100) # white\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 time.sleep(0.05)\r\n\r\nprint(\"Done!\")\r\ndrone.close()<\/pre>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2213b754447\" name=\"6a2213b754447\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 9<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Using Gyroscope with Functions<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]To fly in shape formations or do autonomous flight missions, you will be using the gyroscope a lot. Instead of having to rewrite the turning code over and over, turn it into a function <code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">turn_right_degree(degrees)<\/code> that will take a parameter of how many degrees you want to turn right. <strong>Remember that the gyroscope uses a positive angle for turning left and a negative angle for turning right. This is opposite the flight movements!<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>&nbsp;<\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"python\">def turn_right_degree(degrees):\r\n    z_angle = drone.get_angle_z()\r\n    initial_z_angle = z_angle # Save initial z-angle in a variable\r\n    while z_angle &gt; initial_z_angle - degrees:\r\n        drone.set_yaw(-20) # Use negative power for right turns\r\n        drone.move()\r\n        z_angle = drone.get_angle_z()\r\n\r\ndrone.takeoff()\r\nturn_right_degree(45)\r\ndrone.land()\r\ndrone.close()<\/pre>\n<p>Try changing the parameters for <code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">turn_right_degree()<\/code>. Make sure you stay below 180.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Challenge:<\/strong> Write a function\u00a0<code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">turn_left_degree()<\/code> all on your own![\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2213b7544bf\" name=\"6a2213b7544bf\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 10<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Turn_degree()<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]We provide a function that allows the drone to turn to a given angle, <code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">turn_degree(degrees)<\/code>.\u00a0 This function turns the drone right or left with absolute reference frame to drone&#8217;s initial heading. The drone&#8217;s initial heading is defined (set to 0 degrees) by the drone&#8217;s heading after takeoff or when drone pairs to the controller after powering on.<\/p>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;13360&#8243; img_size=&#8221;large&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;][vc_column_text]Using <code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">turn_degree(45)<\/code> will turn the drone 45 degrees to the left, and using <code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">turn_degree(-45)<\/code> will turn the drone 45 degrees to the right.<\/p>\n<p>What happens if you run the code below?<\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">drone = Drone()\r\ndrone.pair()\r\n\r\ndrone.takeoff()\r\n\r\ndrone.turn_degree(45)\r\ndrone.turn_degree(45)\r\n\r\ndrone.land()\r\ndrone.close()<\/pre>\n<p>You will notice that using <code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">turn_degree(45)<\/code> consecutively will turn the drone 45 degrees to the left, relative to the drone&#8217;s initial heading, once and then do nothing after. Why? This is because, after the drone turns +45 degrees for the first time, the drone is already +45 degrees relative to the drone&#8217;s initial heading! Therefore, the drone does not have to turn again.<\/p>\n<p>Run this code to become familiar with the <code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">turn_degree()<\/code> function:<\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">from codrone_edu.drone import *\r\n\r\ndrone = Drone()\r\ndrone.pair()\r\n\r\ndrone.takeoff()\r\n\r\nangles = [45, 45, -45, 90, 180, -180, 0]\r\n\r\nfor angle in angles:\r\n    print(\"Turn to degree\", angle)\r\n    drone.turn_degree(angle)\r\n\r\ndrone.land()\r\ndrone.close()<\/pre>\n<p><strong>Note: <\/strong>This function only accepts angles from -180 to 180 degrees.[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2213b75451c\" name=\"6a2213b75451c\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 11<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Challenge 1: A More Perfect Polygon<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Use the <code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">turn_degree()<\/code> <\/span><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">and loops to have your drone fly in the shape of your choosing!<\/span><\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>Triangle<\/li>\n<li>Square<\/li>\n<li>Pentagon<\/li>\n<li>Hexagon<\/li>\n<li>Octagon<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2213b754578\" name=\"6a2213b754578\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 12<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Challenge 2: Plinko<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_single_image image=&#8221;3869&#8243; title=&#8221;Plinko Game&#8221;][vc_column_text]You may have seen a plinko board before, if not there is an image example above. A puck is dropped, and it bounces back and forth in a zigzag towards the bottom. For this challenge, you will recreate the downward zigzag motion of a plinko board using the gyroscope.<\/p>\n<p>This program must:<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>Start at a specific height<\/li>\n<li>Drop in a zigzag<\/li>\n<li>Use a loop<\/li>\n<li>Use <span style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">turn_degree()<\/code><\/span><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p>All motions must be equal[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2213b7545c0\" name=\"6a2213b7545c0\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 13<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Lesson Complete<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]In this lesson, you learned about the CoDrone EDU&#8217;s gyroscope sensor. You learned how a gyroscope works, how it reports the drone&#8217;s movements, and how that can be used to control the CoDrone EDU&#8217;s flight movements.[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;7546&#8243; img_size=&#8221;750&#215;670&#8243; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row]<\/p>\n<\/div>","protected":false},"excerpt":{"rendered":"[vc_row][vc_column] Step 14 Before You Fly [vc_column_text css=\".vc_custom_1730227373096{background-color: #ffdddd !important;border-radius: 3px !important;}\"] PRE-FLIGHT WARNING Our drones are durable, yet not indestructible. Please exercise caution while piloting your drone. To prolong&nbsp;<a href=\"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/lesson\/3-4-gyroscope-cde\/\">&hellip;<\/a>","protected":false},"featured_media":4735,"template":"","meta":{"_initial_content":"","_new_post":false,"_quiz_has_questions":false,"_lesson_complexity":"","_lesson_length":10,"_lesson_course":3875,"_lesson_preview":"preview"},"lesson-category":[1782,1848,1770,1793,1826,1482,853,694,59,171,63],"lesson-tag":[4328,14401,2162,2161,2156,2122,2134,2155],"class_list":["post-4420","lesson","type-lesson","status-publish","has-post-thumbnail","hentry","lesson-tag-cde","l